Two quick thoughts:
I agree that the space-space version (i.e., with projected factors as random effects) is often more computationally efficient, but also doesn't apply in the rank-deficient case, so in dsem/tinyVAST etc I allow the user to instead select the MVN on unprojected factors and then projecting them afterwards, e.g., using `dsem_control(  "gmrf_parameterization" )` ;
Zheng et al. In review here (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.14185) has demo code for computing the diagonal of the hat matrix for GLMMs with continuous responses which it then uses to compute effective degrees of freedom (I think it's Eq-4 and associated code) ... am I right in guessing that you're only interested in the diagonal of the hat matrix, or the full matrix?  I haven't actually compared the calculation with conventional leverage metrics though.  And Nan would be the expert to address follow up questions on that
Jim

